# MKIMG -- build the RealXV6 BOOT FLOPPY on RealXV6.  The step after mkkern:
# where mkkern rebuilds the kernel and folds it into a .COM, this lays that
# .COM onto the medium the machine boots from.  Driven by drun:
#
#     cd /src/sys ; drun mkimg
#
# The on-target version of the host build that laid this tree's own floppy,
# and the layout has nothing to it:
#
#     720 zero sectors, boot.com at sector 0, unix.com at sector 1
#
# The boot sector is ASSEMBLED HERE, not carried: BOOT/BOOTSEC.A is the
# DeSmet ASM88 port of it, and asm88 + dbind + mkboot -s turn that into the
# 512 bytes the BIOS loads.  mkboot -s does the last cut (strip the MZ
# header, zero-fill to 510, stamp 55 AA) because ASM88 has no ORG directive,
# so the signature has no spelling in the source.
#
# BOOT.COM is the older build of that same sector, from the toolchain the
# port was made from, and is kept only as the golden to dcmp against.
# IDENTICAL there means the DeSmet assembler reproduced it byte for byte,
# and the floppy below is then built from the sector THIS MACHINE
# assembled, not from the carried one.
#
# What that sector does: read floppy sectors 1..119 into 1000h:0100h and
# far-jump there with SS = 1000h -- exactly the .COM convention e2c targets,
# which is why the DeSmet kernel needs no adapting to it.
#
# BOOTL passes -L../rt/ for the same reason KERNL does, even though a boot
# sector references nothing in the library: BIND opens CSTDIO.S regardless,
# and with _MSDOS2_ = 0 it never reaches findfile(), so it opens the bare
# name in the current directory.  On DOS the PATH hid this (C:\DESMET\LIB is
# on it); here the first run died with "cannot open CSTDIO.S / BIND
# abandoned".  Nothing is pulled from it -- the object comes out the same
# 114 bytes either way -- it just has to be openable.
#
# The kernel input is /test/REFK.COM, not the UNIX.COM mkkern leaves in this
# directory, and that is deliberate:
#
#   * it is the same golden mkkern's last step proves the target's own
#     rebuild equal to, so "mkimg made a floppy of REFK.COM" plus "mkkern
#     made a UNIX.COM IDENTICAL to REFK.COM" is the whole claim, without
#     this script depending on a 30-minute kernel build having just run;
#   * space.  The floppy is 720 blocks and a fresh image has about 900
#     free; mkkern's own objects and UNIX.EXE take roughly 460 of those, so
#     the two do not fit in one boot unless the intermediates are removed
#     first.  Keeping them apart keeps both well inside the disk.
#
# mkboot verifies what it wrote by reading the whole image back and
# comparing it against the two inputs and against zero, so a short write --
# the realistic failure here, this being 720 blocks into a filesystem with
# ~900 free -- fails loudly instead of a floppy that dies in the BIOS.
# That is self-consistency, though, so the layout itself is checked against
# a HOST-built golden first: the same three placements (boot sector at 0,
# payload at 1, zero fill to the end) in an 8-sector image, which dcmp can
# compare byte for byte for 8 blocks instead of 720.  /test/REFF.IMG is the
# host build of that same layout, so IDENTICAL means the target's mkboot
# and the host's agree on the format.  Its payload is the boot sector again
# -- any file drives the same code path, and this one is already here.
asm88 BOOT/BOOTSEC
dbind -fBOOTL
mkboot -s BOOTSEC.EXE BOOTSEC.COM
dcmp BOOTSEC.COM BOOT.COM
mkboot BOOTSEC.COM BOOTSEC.COM /tmp/TINY.IMG 8
dcmp /tmp/TINY.IMG /test/REFF.IMG
mkboot BOOTSEC.COM /test/REFK.COM /tmp/UNIX360.IMG
echo === MKIMG FLOPPY OK ===
